{"text": "\u00ab cHILIP MORRIS USA INTER-OFFICE CORRESPONDENCE\n120 PARK AVENUE, NEW YORK, NY 10017-8592\nTo: Distribution DATE: May 13, 1997\n\nMarketing Information & Analysis/Ellen Fitzgerald\nE MARGIN COMPETITIVE IPON ACTIVITY\nAttached is the latest competitive supermarket coupon information (through March 24,\n1991) as reported by various data sources. Note: Media-delivered couponing\ninformation as reported by the Advertising Checking Bureau is currently available\nthrough February.\n\u2018SUMMARY\n\n+ RJR reduced the overall level of coupon support behind their Full Margin brands.\nThis shift was driven by reductions in on-carton/in-store couponing.\n\n* Philip Morris\u2019 Full Margin couponing increased versus a year-ago due to the various\nFourth Quarter 1990 Direct Mail programs for Marlboro, Merit and Benson & Hedges.\n\n+ Lorillard significantly increased coupon support behind Newport, Kent and True.\nImportantly, this higher value coupon activity has had a positive impact on the\nbrands\u2019 share performance in the supermarket trade class (as reported by the\nInfoScan Rapid Fact data base). In addition, Lorillard is using a new coupon delivery\nsystem: Register Receipt Coupons which delivers $5.00 coupons for Newport to\n\u2018consumers at retail,\n\n+ American and Brown & Williamson are focusing their couponing efforts behind their\nPrice Value/Sub-Generic brands and have consequently reduced support for the\nmanufacturers\u2019 Full Margin businesses.\n\nHIGHLIGHTS\n\n+ Supermarket Full Margin couponing trends continue to be driven primarily by R.J,\nReynolds (40% of volume sold on coupon) and Lorillard's recent emphasis behind\ntheir Full Margin brands (37% of volume) for the four-week period ending March\n24th, However, note that RJR\u2019s Full Margin couponing has lost momentum during\nFirst Quarter 1991. Full Margin\u2019s percent volume sold on coupon for the\nmanufacturer is down from a high of 51% at the end of 1990 to its current level of\n40%, Price Value couponing for RW. Reynolds remained stable at around 62%\nduring First Quarter 1991.\n\n+ Couponing for RJR's Full Margin brands declined primarily as a result of decreasing\nin-store/on-carton coupon activity. For the most current four-week period, the percent\nof stores with on-carton couponing for Winston decreased -24 points to 14% versus\nthe prior period; Salem, -27 points to 16%; Camel, -27 points to 17%; and Vantage,\n-24 points to 18%.\n\nWith the exception of Camel, however, Full Margin coupon activity (supported by\nmedia-delivered programs) for Winston (42% of volume sold on coupon), Salem\n(45%) and Vantage (57%) remained high relative to the industry average. Camel\nexperienced a significant decline in volume sold on coupon (-16 points to only 19%)\ndue in part to reductions after the introductory support behind Ultra Lights.\nAdditionally, it is likely that RJR is reducing the level of in-store/on-carton couponing\nbehind the brand to refocus support to Camel on pack sales in Convenience Outlets.\n\nTSL9OZEVOS",
